### What happens when you use a random UUID as a primary key?

A database primary-key index is a B-tree sorted by the key. When the key is monotonically increasing (1, 2, 3, or a time-ordered id), every new row's key is larger than all existing keys, so it appends to the rightmost leaf page. That page stays hot in memory and fills up neatly. A random UUIDv4 has no order, so each new value lands at a random position in the B-tree, requiring insertion into the middle of the tree.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;

@Entity
@Table(name = "transactions")
public class Transaction {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.UUID)
    private java.util.UUID id;   // random UUIDv4 -> scatters across B-tree

    @Column(name = "account_id")
    private String accountId;

    @Column(name = "amount", precision = 15, scale = 2)
    private java.math.BigDecimal amount;
}
```

```text
Random UUIDv4 inserts:
- id = 3f2a... -> insert into MIDDLE of B-tree
- id = a8e1... -> insert into a DIFFERENT middle position
- id = 1b7c... -> insert into yet ANOTHER position
- Each insert targets a random leaf page
```

### What is a B-tree page split?

When a new key must be inserted into a leaf page that is already full, the database performs a page split: it allocates a new page and moves roughly half the rows across to make room. Page splits are expensive because they require extra I/O, extra locking, and leave both the old and new pages approximately half-full.

```text
Sequential keys (no splits):
  Page 1: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]     Page 2: [6, 7, 8, 9, 10]    Page 3: [11, 12, ...]  <- append
  All pages full, no splits.

Random UUIDv4 keys (constant splits):
  Page 1: [1b7c, 3f2a, ...]   <- FULL, new key 2e9f must go here
  SPLIT! Page 1 becomes:       Page 1a: [1b7c, 2e9f]   Page 1b: [3f2a, ...]
  Both pages now HALF-FULL -> wasted space = fragmentation
```

### What is index fragmentation?

After many page splits, the index contains many half-full pages scattered across disk. The index occupies far more pages than the data actually needs, wasting both disk and memory. Scans that read the index sequentially must jump between non-contiguous pages, making range queries slower.

```text
Fragmentation comparison (1M rows):
  Sequential PK: 5,000 pages, 95% full, contiguous on disk
  Random UUID PK: 12,000 pages, 50% full, scattered on disk
  -> 2.4x more pages, 2.4x more I/O for full scans
```

### Why is it worse on InnoDB?

InnoDB uses a clustered index where the primary key physically orders the table rows themselves. The row data lives in the leaf pages of the primary-key B-tree. So a random primary key scatters the ACTUAL ROWS, not just a secondary index. Additionally, every secondary index in InnoDB stores the full primary key as its row pointer, so a 16-byte random UUID bloats every secondary index.

```text
InnoDB clustered index:
  PK = row ordering. Random PK = random row order.
  Secondary index stores: [indexed_column, PK]
  With UUID PK: [account_id, 16-byte-UUID] per entry
  With BIGINT PK: [account_id, 8-byte-BIGINT] per entry
  -> Every secondary index is 2x larger with UUID

PostgreSQL heap tables:
  Rows stored in heap (insertion order), PK is a separate B-tree.
  Random PK fragments the INDEX but not the ROW storage.
  Still bad, but less severe than InnoDB.
```

### How do you fix it with UUIDv7 or ULID?

Use a time-ordered identifier so inserts append near the end while keeping global uniqueness. UUIDv7 encodes a millisecond timestamp in the high bits. ULID is a 128-bit, lexicographically sortable, timestamp-prefixed id.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import org.hibernate.annotations.UuidGenerator;

@Entity
@Table(name = "transactions")
public class Transaction {

    // Time-ordered UUIDv7: inserts append, no fragmentation
    @Id
    @UuidGenerator(style = UuidGenerator.Style.TIME)
    private java.util.UUID id;

    @Column(name = "account_id")
    private String accountId;

    @Column(name = "amount", precision = 15, scale = 2)
    private java.math.BigDecimal amount;
}
```

```text
UUIDv7 values generated over time:
  2026-01-15T10:00:00 -> 01930a5f-...
  2026-01-15T10:00:01 -> 01930a60-...
  2026-01-15T10:00:02 -> 01930a61-...
  -> Values sort in time order -> inserts APPEND near the end
  -> No page splits, no fragmentation, cache stays hot
```

### What is the dual-id pattern?

If you need a random, non-guessable identifier for external exposure (API URLs, payment references), keep a sequential or time-ordered clustered PK internally for storage efficiency and expose a separate random UUID as the public id with its own index.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.util.UUID;

@Entity
@Table(name = "payments")
public class Payment {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE, generator = "pay_seq")
    @SequenceGenerator(name = "pay_seq", sequenceName = "payment_seq", allocationSize = 50)
    private Long id;                             // internal clustered PK: sequential, fast

    @Column(name = "public_ref", unique = true, nullable = false, updatable = false)
    private UUID publicRef = UUID.randomUUID();  // external: random, non-guessable

    @Column(name = "amount", precision = 15, scale = 2)
    private java.math.BigDecimal amount;
}
```

```text
Dual-id benefits:
- Internal PK (BIGINT): sequential, small, fast inserts, small secondary indexes
- Public ref (UUID): random, non-guessable, used in URLs and APIs
- Best of both worlds
```

### How do sequential, UUIDv7, and UUIDv4 compare?

| Aspect | Sequential BIGINT | UUIDv7 | UUIDv4 (random) |
|---|---|---|---|
| Insert pattern | Append (rightmost) | Append (roughly) | Random (scattered) |
| Page splits | None | Rare | Frequent |
| Fragmentation | None | Minimal | Severe |
| Cache locality | Excellent | Good | Poor |
| Index size | 8 bytes | 16 bytes | 16 bytes |
| Distributed gen | No (single sequence) | Yes | Yes |
| Guessable | Yes (sequential) | Partially | No |

```java
// Sequential: fastest inserts, not distributed
@Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE, generator = "s")
@SequenceGenerator(name = "s", sequenceName = "txn_seq", allocationSize = 50)
private Long id;

// UUIDv7: append-friendly, distributed, slightly larger
@Id @UuidGenerator(style = UuidGenerator.Style.TIME)
private java.util.UUID id;

// UUIDv4: scattered, DO NOT use as clustered PK
@Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.UUID)
private java.util.UUID id;
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer never uses random UUIDv4 as a clustered primary key on write-heavy tables. They choose UUIDv7 when distributed generation is needed and sequential BIGINT when it is not. They understand that the damage is worst on InnoDB because the clustered index IS the row storage. They use the dual-id pattern when they need both fast inserts and non-guessable external identifiers.

```yaml
# Monitor index fragmentation in PostgreSQL
# SELECT schemaname, tablename, pg_size_pretty(pg_total_relation_size(schemaname||'.'||tablename))
# FROM pg_tables WHERE tablename = 'transactions';

# Monitor in MySQL InnoDB
# SELECT table_name, data_free, data_length
# FROM information_schema.tables WHERE table_name = 'transactions';
```
